window.SKIM_PAPERS["1508.07909"] = {"concepts": [{"id": "bpe-merge-algorithm", "name": "BPE Merge Algorithm", "tier": "minor", "parent": null, "summary": "A greedy loop that builds a subword vocabulary by repeatedly merging the most frequent adjacent symbol pair in the training corpus. Adapted from Gage's 1994 byte-pair compression algorithm, with characters in place of bytes.", "explanation": "The algorithm starts from characters. Every word in the training corpus is represented as a sequence of characters, with a special end-of-word symbol '·' appended to each word so that the original tokenization can be restored after decoding. The symbol vocabulary is initialized to the character vocabulary.\n\nThen it merges, greedily, for a fixed number of iterations. Each iteration counts the frequency of every adjacent symbol pair across the corpus, and replaces every occurrence of the most frequent pair with a single new symbol: pick $(A, B) = \\arg\\max_{(x,y)} \\mathrm{count}(x, y)$, then rewrite each occurrence of the bigram $A\\,B$ as the merged symbol $AB$.\n\nEach merge adds exactly one new symbol, and that symbol represents a character n-gram. Run the loop long enough and frequent n-grams, and eventually whole frequent words, become single symbols.\n\nOne property keeps the loop cheap. Pairs never cross word boundaries, so the counting and rewriting can run on a word-frequency dictionary extracted from the text rather than on the text itself, with each word's pair counts weighted by that word's corpus frequency.\n\nThe paper gives a minimal Python implementation. get_stats counts weighted pairs over the space-separated symbol dictionary, merge_vocab rewrites the winning pair with a whitespace-boundary regex, and the outer loop repeats num_merges times, recording each chosen pair in order.\n\nThat ordered list of merges is the learned model. A toy run on {low, lowest, newer, wider} learns merges such as r · -> r·, l o -> lo, lo w -> low, e r· -> er·.", "prerequisites": [], "sectionIds": [], "floor": false, "citedFrom": null, "sourceNote": "Section 3.2 and Algorithm 1 (page 4), with the toy example in Figure 1.", "ownerPaper": "1508.07909", "sources": {"paperId": "1508.07909", "note": "Section 3.2 and Algorithm 1 (page 4), with the toy example in Figure 1.", "sections": [], "pages": []}}, {"id": "bpe-segmentation-at-test-time", "name": "Applying BPE Merges to New Text", "tier": "minor", "parent": null, "summary": "At test time, words are split into characters and the learned merge operations are replayed in order, producing a segmentation of any word into known subword symbols. This makes a fixed vocabulary effectively open.", "explanation": "Learning BPE and applying BPE are separate steps. The merge list is learned once from training data; segmentation afterwards just replays it.\n\nAny word — including one never seen in training — is first split into its character sequence, with the end-of-word marker attached. The learned merges are then applied in order, combining characters into progressively larger known symbols. In the paper's toy example, the out-of-vocabulary word 'lower' comes out as 'low er·' under merges learned from {low, lowest, newer, wider}.\n\nEvery symbol produced this way is by construction a member of the fixed symbol vocabulary. The model therefore never emits or receives an UNK token for words made of known characters, and the paper reports zero unknown tokens on its test set with per-language BPE (Table 1).\n\nTwo theoretical gaps remain. A word may contain a character not seen in training, or a symbol may have had every one of its training occurrences merged into something larger. The authors observed neither at test time, and note the issue could be fixed by recursively reversing specific merges.\n\nThis property is what lets a translation model with a modest fixed vocabulary handle open-vocabulary input. It is also why downstream systems that report a 'BPE vocabulary' of size V mean this fixed symbol set, not a word list.", "prerequisites": [], "sectionIds": [], "floor": false, "citedFrom": null, "sourceNote": "Section 3.2, paragraph following Algorithm 1, including footnote 3; UNK counts in Table 1 (Section 4.1).", "ownerPaper": "1508.07909", "sources": {"paperId": "1508.07909", "note": "Section 3.2, paragraph following Algorithm 1, including footnote 3; UNK counts in Table 1 (Section 4.1).", "sections": [], "pages": []}}, {"id": "bpe-vocabulary-size", "name": "Vocabulary Size as the Single Hyperparameter", "tier": "minor", "parent": null, "summary": "The final BPE symbol vocabulary size equals the initial character vocabulary size plus the number of merge operations. The merge count is the algorithm's only hyperparameter, so the vocabulary size is chosen directly.", "explanation": "Every merge operation creates exactly one new symbol. So after $k$ merges the symbol vocabulary has size $|V| = |V_{\\mathrm{chars}}| + k$, where $V_{\\mathrm{chars}}$ is the set of characters in the training data.\n\nChoosing $k$ therefore sets the vocabulary size directly. And $k$ is the only knob the method has: there is no shortlist, no frequency cutoff, and no separate segmentation model to tune.\n\nThe choice trades two quantities against each other. A smaller $k$ leaves symbols short and token sequences long. A larger $k$ folds frequent words into single symbols and shortens sequences.\n\nIn its own experiments the paper runs 59,500 merges for per-language BPE and 89,500 for joint BPE. In practice the network vocabulary comes out slightly smaller than the symbol count, because the authors drop infrequent subword symbols that arise from noise such as foreign-alphabet characters.\n\nSo when a later system says it used 'BPE with a vocabulary of about 37,000 tokens', that number is the direct product of this choice of merge count over its own training corpus.", "prerequisites": [], "sectionIds": [], "floor": false, "citedFrom": null, "sourceNote": "Section 3.2 (final-vocabulary-size statement); merge counts and type counts in Section 4.1 and Table 1.", "ownerPaper": "1508.07909", "sources": {"paperId": "1508.07909", "note": "Section 3.2 (final-vocabulary-size statement); merge counts and type counts in Section 4.1 and Table 1.", "sections": [], "pages": []}}, {"id": "joint-bpe-shared-vocabulary", "name": "Joint BPE and the Shared Source-Target Vocabulary", "tier": "minor", "parent": null, "summary": "Joint BPE learns one set of merge operations on the union (in practice, the concatenation) of the source and target training text, giving both languages a single shared subword vocabulary and consistent segmentations.", "explanation": "The paper evaluates two ways to apply BPE to a translation pair. One is to learn two independent encodings, one per language. The other is to learn a single encoding on the union of the two vocabularies, which it calls joint BPE — implemented by simply concatenating the source and target sides of the training set before learning merges.\n\nIndependent encodings have genuine advantages. They are more compact, and they guarantee that every subword was seen in text of its own language.\n\nTheir weakness is consistency. The same string can be segmented differently in the two languages: the paper shows the name Mirzayeva split as Mirz|ayeva on the English side but Mir|za|eva on the Russian side under independent BPE. That mismatch makes the subword-to-subword mapping harder to learn and causes copy and transliteration errors.\n\nJoint BPE makes the segmentations consistent, and in the paper's analysis it improves both precision and recall on out-of-vocabulary words relative to independent BPE. The cost is that joint BPE can produce a few segments that occur only in one language's text — 0.05% of test tokens in the paper's data.\n\nA shared vocabulary buys one more thing. It is what makes it possible for an encoder-decoder model to share one embedding matrix between source input, target input, and output softmax.\n\nFor English-German the paper uses joint BPE with 89,500 merges. Learning one joint BPE model over a concatenated English-German WMT corpus is exactly how later work builds a shared source-target subword vocabulary of a chosen size.", "prerequisites": [], "sectionIds": [], "floor": false, "citedFrom": null, "sourceNote": "Section 3.2, final paragraph (joint BPE definition, footnotes 4-5); consistency analysis in Section 5.2 and Tables 4-5; BPE-J90k results in Tables 2-3.", "ownerPaper": "1508.07909", "sources": {"paperId": "1508.07909", "note": "Section 3.2, final paragraph (joint BPE definition, footnotes 4-5); consistency analysis in Section 5.2 and Tables 4-5; BPE-J90k results in Tables 2-3.", "sections": [], "pages": []}}, {"id": "vocabulary-vs-sequence-length-tradeoff", "name": "Vocabulary Size vs. Sequence Length Trade-off", "tier": "minor", "parent": null, "summary": "Subword segmentation trades vocabulary size against tokenized sequence length: finer splits shrink the vocabulary but lengthen every sentence, and encoder-decoder cost grows at least linearly with sequence length.", "explanation": "The paper frames the choice of segmentation as a two-sided efficiency problem.\n\nOn one side, neural models want a small vocabulary, for time and space efficiency and to avoid back-off dictionaries. On the other, they want compact text, because longer token sequences slow training and decoding and increase the distances over which the network must pass information. The authors note that the time complexity of encoder-decoder architectures is at least linear in sequence length, so oversplitting harms efficiency directly.\n\nCharacter-level segmentation sits at one extreme. On the paper's 100M-token German corpus, full character segmentation gives a vocabulary of just 3,000 types — but it inflates the corpus from 100M to 550M tokens.\n\nBPE sits near the other end. With about 60K merged types the same corpus comes to 112M tokens, roughly a 12% length increase over word-level tokenization, while still leaving zero unknown tokens. The number of merges positions a system anywhere on the curve between those two points.\n\nThis trade-off is why the tokenizer choice directly determines the sequence length n that appears in any per-layer or per-step cost analysis of a sequence model. The same sentence has a different n under different segmentations, and BPE is chosen precisely to keep n close to the word count while keeping the vocabulary small and closed.", "prerequisites": [], "sectionIds": [], "floor": false, "citedFrom": null, "sourceNote": "Section 3.1 (last two paragraphs) and Section 4.1 with Table 1; footnote 8 for the linear-in-length complexity remark.", "ownerPaper": "1508.07909", "sources": {"paperId": "1508.07909", "note": "Section 3.1 (last two paragraphs) and Section 4.1 with Table 1; footnote 8 for the linear-in-length complexity remark.", "sections": [], "pages": []}}], "items": [], "edges": [], "themes": [], "pages": [], "narrative": null, "insights": null, "reading": [], "refs": {"accessed": []}, "regions": [], "pdfPages": 0, "readerKind": "pdf", "sections": []};
